// Fresh Question Bank - Domain 2.0 Threats, Vulnerabilities, and Mitigations (part A: 2.1-2.3)
window.FRESH_BANK = (window.FRESH_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 2.1 Threat actors and motivations (20) ----------
{o:"2.1",q:"Over 18 months, a custom implant quietly copies only missile-guidance design files from a defense contractor's engineering network. Nothing is encrypted, leaked, or offered for sale, and the toolkit includes two zero-day exploits. Which actor is MOST likely responsible?",c:["Hacktivist group seeking publicity for a cause","Organized crime syndicate seeking resale profit","Nation-state group pursuing long-term espionage","Malicious insider seeking personal revenge"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Hacktivists publicize their results and rarely hide for 18 months. This would fit if the stolen files had been dumped online with a political statement attached.","Incorrect. Criminal groups monetize quickly through ransom or resale, and nothing here was sold or encrypted. It would fit if the files had appeared on a dark web marketplace.","Correct. Long dwell time, zero-days, and theft of one narrow category of defense data with no monetization point to a well-funded state-sponsored APT whose motive is espionage.","Incorrect. An insider would already hold legitimate access and would not need zero-day exploits. It would fit if a disgruntled engineer with badge access had copied the files."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"A group floods a city government website the day of a contested zoning vote and posts a manifesto urging council members to reject the measure. No payment is requested and no data is taken. What is the MOST likely motivation?",c:["Philosophical or political belief","Financial gain through extortion","Ethical exposure of wrongdoing","Espionage on council deliberations"],a:0,e:["Correct. Timing the attack to a vote and publishing a manifesto that argues for a policy outcome shows an ideological or political motive, typical of hacktivism.","Incorrect. Extortion requires a demand for money and a threat to continue. It would fit if the group had emailed the city a Bitcoin address and promised to stop the flood after payment.","Incorrect. Ethical motivation means exposing specific misconduct the public deserves to know about. It would fit if the group had leaked documents proving officials hid a violation.","Incorrect. Espionage depends on quietly collecting information, whereas a flood is loud and public. It would fit if the attackers had silently read closed-session email."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"Marketing staff, frustrated that the approved file-sharing platform is slow before a campaign deadline, start using a personal cloud storage account and upload customer lists. They have no intent to harm the company. How is this BEST described?",c:["Organized crime targeting customer data","Insider threat seeking financial gain","Hacktivism against corporate policy","Shadow IT adopted by staff for speed"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Organized crime is an external group with criminal intent. It would fit if outsiders had compromised the personal account to harvest the lists for sale.","Incorrect. A malicious insider acts deliberately for personal profit. It would fit if a marketer had copied the lists to sell them to a competitor.","Incorrect. Hacktivism pursues a cause through disruption or exposure. It would fit if staff had published the lists to protest the company's policies.","Correct. Using unsanctioned technology to get work done, without malicious intent, is shadow IT. The risk is unmanaged data exposure outside corporate controls."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"A senior administrator is passed over for promotion, then deletes production backups on his last day before resigning. He copies no data and demands nothing. Which actor and motivation fit BEST?",c:["Insider threat motivated by data exfiltration","Insider threat motivated by personal revenge","Unskilled attacker motivated by disruption","Organized crime motivated by financial gain"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Exfiltration means copying data out, and he copied nothing. It would fit if he had taken the backups to a competitor instead of deleting them.","Correct. He used legitimate access (insider) and the timing after the missed promotion, with destruction rather than theft or payment, shows revenge as the motive.","Incorrect. An unskilled attacker uses others' tools from outside, with no privileged access. It would fit if an outsider ran a downloaded wiper tool for fun.","Incorrect. Criminal groups act for payment. It would fit if the backups had been encrypted and a ransom demanded."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"A group with a help desk, a revenue-sharing affiliate program, and professional negotiators encrypts a hospital's servers and demands cryptocurrency. After payment it supplies a decryptor and takes no further interest in the hospital. Which actor BEST fits?",c:["Hacktivist collective with a political agenda","Nation-state intelligence service","Insider with a grudge against the hospital","Organized crime ransomware operation"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Hacktivists seek publicity for a cause and rarely run negotiation desks. It would fit if the group had posted demands about healthcare policy instead of asking for payment.","Incorrect. A state service would pursue espionage or strategic disruption and would not walk away after being paid. It would fit if the intrusion had stayed hidden to collect patient intelligence.","Incorrect. An insider would not need affiliates or a negotiation desk. It would fit if one employee had wiped systems out of personal anger.","Correct. A business-like structure, affiliate revenue sharing, and a payment-for-decryptor model identify organized crime, whose motive is financial gain."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"A teenager downloads a point-and-click DDoS tool from a forum and knocks a small game server offline to see whether it works. No demand or statement follows. Which actor and motivation fit BEST?",c:["Hacktivist, protesting the publisher's policies","Unskilled attacker, curiosity and disruption","Organized crime, extorting the server owner","Nation-state, testing offensive capabilities"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Hacktivists publish a message tied to a cause. It would fit if the attack had come with a statement criticizing the publisher.","Correct. Someone using pre-built tools with little skill is an unskilled attacker, and trying it for the sake of it makes curiosity and disruption the motive.","Incorrect. Extortion needs a demand backed by a threat. It would fit if the attacker had emailed the owner asking for payment to stop the attack.","Incorrect. A state actor would use custom tooling against strategic targets. It would fit if the target were a government network and the tooling were bespoke."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"Attackers quietly copy unpublished clinical-trial data from a drugmaker and pass it to a competitor racing for the same approval. The victim never receives a demand. What is the motivation?",c:["Espionage for a competitor's benefit","Extortion for a large ransom payment","Revenge for a past commercial dispute","Disruption of the victim's operations"],a:0,e:["Correct. Stealing confidential information to give a competitor an edge is espionage. The absence of a demand and the competitor hand-off both support it.","Incorrect. Extortion requires a demand to the victim. It would fit if the attackers had threatened to publish the data unless paid.","Incorrect. Revenge is a personal grievance with the victim. It would fit if a former partner had destroyed the data after a contract dispute.","Incorrect. Disruption aims to take systems offline, but nothing here was disturbed. It would fit if the trial database had been encrypted or deleted."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"After copying a CEO's private emails, an attacker writes: send 20 BTC by Friday or the messages go to the press. The emails contain nothing illegal. Which motivation is this?",c:["Espionage to gain strategic advantage","Ethical exposure of executive misconduct","Blackmail to extract money under threat","Revenge against the CEO personally"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Espionage gathers information quietly for advantage and makes no demand. It would fit if the attacker had passed the emails to a competitor.","Incorrect. Ethical motivation exposes real wrongdoing. It would fit if the emails proved fraud and the attacker published them without asking for money.","Correct. Demanding payment under threat of exposure is blackmail. The emails hold no misconduct, so there is no public-interest justification.","Incorrect. Revenge would be personal and need no payment demand. It would fit if the attacker leaked the emails to hurt the CEO with no ask."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"An analyst's report labels a suspect as holding a valid badge and VPN account, versus an outsider with no prior access to the network. Which threat actor attribute is she recording?",c:["Level of technical sophistication","Internal versus external position","Amount of funding and resources","Intent and motivation of the actor"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Sophistication describes technical skill and tooling. It would be the right attribute if the report discussed custom malware versus public scripts.","Correct. Whether an actor already has legitimate access inside the organization or operates from outside is the internal versus external attribute.","Incorrect. Resources describe money, people, and infrastructure. It would be the right attribute if the report compared a lone hobbyist with a state-backed team.","Incorrect. Intent describes why the actor attacks. It would be the right attribute if the report discussed profit versus protest."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"Which characteristic BEST distinguishes a nation-state actor from most other threat actors?",c:["Operating primarily from inside the target organization","Preference for publicly available scripts and tools","Heavy reliance on phishing as the initial access method","Long-term, well-funded campaigns with strategic goals"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Operating from inside describes insider threats. Nation-states are generally external, though they may recruit insiders.","Incorrect. Reliance on public tools describes unskilled attackers. It would apply to script kiddies who lack the means to build their own.","Incorrect. Nearly every actor uses phishing, so it does not distinguish anyone. It would only matter in comparing initial access methods.","Correct. Large budgets, custom tooling, and long-term goals such as espionage or pre-positioning in infrastructure separate state actors from the rest."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"A group leaks documents proving a chemical company hid specific safety violations, stating the public deserves to know. It makes no demands and calls for no change in government policy. Which motivation applies BEST?",c:["Ethical exposure of hidden wrongdoing","Espionage on behalf of a competitor","Philosophical or political advocacy","Financial gain from selling the documents"],a:0,e:["Correct. Disclosing specific, real misconduct because the public has a right to know is ethical motivation, distinct from a general political cause.","Incorrect. Espionage delivers information privately to a beneficiary. It would fit if the documents had gone quietly to a rival chemical firm.","Incorrect. Political advocacy promotes a broad cause or policy. It would fit if the group had used the leak to demand new legislation.","Incorrect. Financial gain would involve a sale or ransom. It would fit if the group had auctioned the documents to the highest bidder."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"During an armed conflict, attackers trip breakers at several substations just before a ground offensive and never claim responsibility. Which motivation fits BEST?",c:["Political protest against the government","Espionage on grid operations data","Warfare supporting military objectives","Disruption of services for notoriety"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Protest groups publicize their actions and demands. It would fit if a group had claimed credit and listed political grievances.","Incorrect. Espionage observes quietly and avoids damage. It would fit if the attackers had only mapped the grid and left it running.","Correct. Targeting critical infrastructure in step with kinetic operations, with no claim of credit, serves a military objective, which is war as a motive.","Incorrect. Notoriety seekers want attention and credit. It would fit if the outage had been followed by a boastful post under a handle."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"Which scenario is an example of insider data exfiltration?",c:["Plugging a found USB drive into a work laptop out of simple curiosity","Deleting a project folder out of anger after a poor performance review","Sharing a password with a teammate who needs urgent access to meet a deadline","Emailing the customer list to a personal account before joining a rival"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Plugging in a found drive is a baiting scenario, a negligent act rather than data theft. It would matter only if the drive then copied data off the system.","Incorrect. Deleting data is destruction, an attack on availability motivated by revenge. It would count as exfiltration only if the data had been copied out.","Incorrect. Sharing credentials is a policy violation that creates risk but does not move data out. It would be exfiltration only if the colleague were an outsider taking data.","Correct. Moving data out of the organization to personal storage, whether for self-interest or a new employer, is exfiltration."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"A contractor's project ends, but nobody disables the VPN account issued to him. Three months later he logs in with those credentials and browses file shares. How is he BEST classified?",c:["Unskilled attacker using a leaked tool","Insider threat abusing unrevoked access","Shadow IT through unsanctioned remote access","Hacktivist exploiting a former client"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. An unskilled attacker has no valid credentials and relies on public tools. It would fit if an outsider had used a downloaded script against the VPN gateway.","Correct. Holding company-issued credentials that were never deprovisioned makes him an insider, and the remedy is timely offboarding and access reviews.","Incorrect. Shadow IT is unapproved technology adopted by staff. It would fit if the contractor had set up his own remote desktop tool without approval.","Incorrect. Hacktivism requires a cause. It would fit if he had defaced pages and published a statement."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"Why are malicious insiders often harder to detect than external attackers?",c:["Custom zero-day tooling evades signature-based antivirus engines","Perimeter firewalls inspect only inbound sessions from the internet","Valid credentials make malicious actions look like routine work","Encrypted tunnels hide their sessions from endpoint logging"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Evasive custom tooling is characteristic of advanced external actors. It would explain detection difficulty for an APT, but insiders rarely need exploits.","Incorrect. Firewalls typically inspect both directions, and inspection direction is not the reason insiders succeed. It would matter in a discussion of egress filtering gaps.","Correct. Insiders use legitimate accounts and permissions, so their activity looks normal. Behavioral analytics, least privilege, and audit review are the usual countermeasures.","Incorrect. Endpoint logging records activity regardless of the tunnel, and insiders do not depend on tunnels. It would matter for external attackers hiding command-and-control traffic."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"A group advertises resold footholds in compromised corporate networks on a dark web forum, with a price list and escrow for any buyer. Which actor BEST fits?",c:["Organized crime running a profit-driven access market","Hacktivist collective funding a political campaign","Insider reselling their employer's credentials","Nation-state operating as a strategic intelligence service"],a:0,e:["Correct. Selling access to any buyer for profit is the initial-access-broker model of organized cybercrime.","Incorrect. Hacktivists seek publicity for a cause rather than operating a storefront. It would fit if they published the access to embarrass targets.","Incorrect. An insider sells access to a single employer, not a catalog of many networks. It would fit if one employee had offered only their own company's VPN.","Incorrect. A state service uses access for its own objectives rather than selling to anyone. It would fit if the access were used exclusively against a defense target."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"An attacker defaces dozens of unrelated websites with memes, makes no demands, and posts no statement of cause. Which motivation BEST fits?",c:["Philosophical or political protest","Chaos with a hunger for notoriety","Ethical exposure of wrongdoing","Espionage on the site owners"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Political protest ties the action to a stated cause. It would fit if the defacements carried slogans supporting a policy position.","Correct. Random targets, no demand, and no message indicate disruption for fun or recognition, which is chaos as a motive.","Incorrect. Ethical motivation exposes specific misconduct. It would fit if each page revealed documented wrongdoing by its owner.","Incorrect. Espionage depends on quiet information collection. It would fit if the attacker had silently copied data from the sites instead of drawing attention."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"One group defaces a ministry site to oppose a new law. Another leaks documents proving a utility concealed water contamination. Which statement BEST distinguishes the two motivations?",c:["Political attacks mostly hit governments, while ethical attacks mostly hit businesses","Ethical attacks are legally authorized, while political attacks are not","Political attacks seek payment from victims, while ethical attacks seek public attention","Political attacks push a broad cause, while ethical attacks expose specific wrongs"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Both motives can target either governments or businesses, as these two scenarios show in reverse. Target type does not define the motive.","Incorrect. Neither attack is authorized, and legality does not separate the motives. Authorization applies to penetration testing, which is a different matter.","Incorrect. Payment is a financial motive and is not what separates these two. It would describe extortion groups instead.","Correct. The first group pushes a cause or policy position, while the second exposes a concrete wrong it believes the public must know about."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"An analyst notes that an actor wrote custom malware, chained two zero-day exploits, and used stealthy persistence. Which attribute describes the technical skill being shown?",c:["Amount of resources and funding","Internal versus external access position","Sophistication and capability level","Intent and motivation of the actor"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Resources describe budget and personnel. It would be the right attribute if the analyst were estimating how much money backed the operation.","Incorrect. Internal versus external describes access relative to the organization. It would be right if the analyst noted the actor held valid credentials.","Correct. Custom tooling, zero-days, and stealth demonstrate high technical skill, which is the sophistication and capability attribute.","Incorrect. Intent is the why behind the attack. It would be right if the analyst were assessing whether the goal was profit or protest."]},
{o:"2.1",q:"A sales representative installs a free browser extension that quietly copies page contents to an outside server. The rep believed it was a productivity tool. How is the rep's action BEST described?",c:["Shadow IT that introduces a malicious vector","Supply chain compromise of an approved vendor","Insider threat executing planned exfiltration","Organized crime targeting the sales team"],a:0,e:["Correct. Installing unapproved software outside IT oversight is shadow IT, and here it opened a malicious data-collection path without the rep intending harm.","Incorrect. Supply chain attacks hit a trusted, approved vendor. It would fit if the company's sanctioned extension had been poisoned upstream.","Incorrect. An insider threat involves deliberate misuse. It would fit if the rep had installed the tool knowing it would siphon data.","Incorrect. Organized crime may have authored the extension, but the stem asks about the rep's action. It would fit if describing the extension's developers."]},

// ---------- 2.2 Threat vectors and attack surfaces (26) ----------
{o:"2.2",q:"An attacker phones the help desk claiming to be the CFO, says a board meeting starts in ten minutes, and pushes the technician to reset the executive's password right now without the usual verification. Which TWO social engineering principles are MOST clearly exploited?",c:["Implied consensus and scarcity","Claimed authority and time urgency","Veiled intimidation and trust","Rapport-based familiarity and liking"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Consensus relies on others supposedly having done the same thing, and scarcity on limited availability. They would fit if the caller said every other technician had already approved similar resets.","Correct. Claiming the CFO's identity invokes authority, and the ten-minute board meeting creates urgency that pressures the technician to skip verification.","Incorrect. Intimidation needs threats or fear, and the caller made none. It would fit if he had warned the technician of being fired for refusing.","Incorrect. Familiarity and liking build rapport over time. They would fit if the caller chatted about shared hobbies and a mutual friend before asking, rather than citing a deadline and a title."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"Engineers at a defense contractor are infected after browsing a legitimate aerospace news site they all bookmarked. The URL was typed correctly, and no email or message was involved. Which attack BEST explains the infections?",c:["Watering hole attack on a compromised trade site","Typosquatting of the trade publication's domain name","Spear phishing with a malicious link sent by email","Pharming through poisoned DNS resolver records"],a:0,e:["Correct. Attackers compromised a real site the target group habitually visits and infected visitors. The correct, bookmarked URL and absence of email point to a watering hole.","Incorrect. Typosquatting depends on mistyped or look-alike domains. It would fit if users had landed on a similar-looking domain after a typing error.","Incorrect. Spear phishing delivers a lure by message to named individuals. It would fit if each engineer had received a tailored email linking to the site.","Incorrect. Pharming redirects correct URLs to fake servers, but here the genuine site served the malware. It would fit if DNS had returned an attacker-controlled address."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"An accounts payable clerk receives an email from a supplier's real address asking to update bank details for the next invoice. The email has no link or attachment, and the supplier's mailbox had been compromised weeks earlier. What attack is this?",c:["Whaling aimed at the supplier's executives","Spear phishing with a malware attachment","Business email compromise payment fraud","Smishing of the clerk's mobile phone"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Whaling targets senior executives, and the victim here is a clerk. It would fit if the message had gone to the supplier's CFO.","Incorrect. Spear phishing usually delivers a payload or credential harvester. It would fit if the email had carried a malicious attachment.","Correct. A hijacked trusted business account used to redirect a payment, with no malware at all, is classic business email compromise.","Incorrect. Smishing is delivered by SMS. It would fit if the bank-detail request had arrived as a text message."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"A user mistypes a well-known search engine's address and lands on a convincing look-alike page that collects credentials. DNS, the registrar, and the real site are all uncompromised. Which technique did the attacker use?",c:["Pharming via DNS cache poisoning","Typosquatting a misspelled domain","Domain hijacking of the registrar record","Watering hole compromise of a popular site"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Pharming sends users who typed the correct name to a fake server by corrupting DNS. It would fit if DNS answers had been altered and the user typed the address correctly.","Correct. Registering a misspelled domain to catch typing errors is typosquatting. Nothing else in the chain was compromised.","Incorrect. Hijacking takes over the genuine domain's registration. It would fit if the real domain's records had been altered at the registrar.","Incorrect. A watering hole infects a legitimate site that targets visit. It would fit if the real site itself were serving malware."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"Employees in one office type their bank's correct URL and reach a fake login page. The internal DNS resolver is returning an IP address that differs from the bank's real one, although other networks resolve correctly. What is this?",c:["Watering hole on the bank's website","Typosquatting with a look-alike domain","On-path ARP spoofing at the access layer","Pharming through poisoning of the DNS cache"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A watering hole compromises the real site, whereas the bank's site is untouched and other networks reach it normally. It would fit if the genuine site served malware.","Incorrect. Typosquatting requires users to enter a different domain. It would fit if the address bar had shown a misspelled name.","Incorrect. ARP spoofing redirects frames at layer 2, while here the DNS answer itself is wrong. It would fit if the resolver's answers were correct but traffic was still intercepted.","Correct. The resolver is handing out a wrong IP for the correct name, which is pharming through DNS cache poisoning. Typing the right URL does not help."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"Thousands of recipients get a text claiming a package is held and asking them to pay a $1.99 redelivery fee through a link. Which term BEST describes this delivery method?",c:["Smishing via mass SMS text messages","Spear phishing via tailored email","Vishing via automated phone call","Whaling via targeted executive messages"],a:0,e:["Correct. Phishing delivered by text message to many numbers is smishing.","Incorrect. Spear phishing is targeted email. It would fit if named employees got tailored emails with the same link.","Incorrect. Vishing uses voice calls. It would fit if the fee request had come through an automated phone call.","Incorrect. Whaling is aimed at senior executives with tailored messages. It would fit if only a handful of C-level targets received personalized lures."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"After studying her LinkedIn profile, an attacker emails a company's CFO a tailored message about an urgent confidential acquisition and asks her to open the attached deal summary. Which term describes this MOST specifically?",c:["Spear phishing against a mid-level employee","Pretexting by unsolicited phone call","Whaling against a senior-level executive","Business email compromise from a vendor"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Spear phishing is targeted, but the victim is a C-level officer. It would be the best term if the target were an ordinary employee.","Incorrect. Pretexting is an invented scenario and this attack is an email, not a call. It would fit if the attacker had phoned with a cover story.","Correct. A tailored lure aimed at a senior executive is whaling, a specialized form of spear phishing.","Incorrect. BEC redirects payments through a trusted business account. It would fit if the email asked for a wire to a changed bank account."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"A caller claims to be from the tax authority, says the employee owes back taxes, and demands gift card codes within the hour or a warrant will be issued. Which combination of delivery method and tactic is shown?",c:["Email phishing impersonating a bank to verify balances","SMS phishing impersonating a vendor under time pressure","Voice phishing building rapport through shared interests","Voice phishing posing as an authority with a deadline"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. This was a call, not an email, and the demand was for gift cards rather than balance verification. It would fit a bank-themed email.","Incorrect. The contact came by voice rather than text, and the caller posed as an authority rather than a vendor. It would fit a smishing text claiming to be a carrier.","Incorrect. The call was by phone, but the caller used threats rather than rapport. It would fit a caller who warmly mentions a mutual acquaintance.","Correct. A phone call (vishing) from a supposed government authority, with a one-hour deadline and threat of arrest, combines impersonation and urgency."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"An attacker scatters USB drives labeled 'Executive Layoffs 2027' in a company parking lot, hoping employees will plug them in at work. Which threat vector does this exploit?",c:["Software supply chain compromise","Removable media baiting scheme","Default credentials on a device","Unsecure wireless network"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Supply chain attacks poison a trusted vendor's product. It would fit if malware had arrived inside a legitimate vendor update.","Correct. Leaving infected removable media to exploit curiosity is baiting through removable devices. Disabling autorun, USB device control, and training reduce the risk.","Incorrect. Default credentials are unchanged logins on a system. It would fit if the attack logged in with admin/admin.","Incorrect. No wireless network is involved. It would fit if employees were lured onto a rogue Wi-Fi network instead."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"Conference-room devices keep Bluetooth discoverable. Attendees repeatedly receive unsolicited pop-up messages on their phones, but no files or contacts are taken. What is this?",c:["Evil twin to impersonate a Wi-Fi network","Bluesnarfing to extract stored data","Bluejacking to send spam messages","War driving to map wireless networks"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An evil twin is a rogue Wi-Fi access point and has nothing to do with Bluetooth. It would fit if a fake SSID had captured traffic.","Incorrect. Bluesnarfing steals data such as contacts without consent. It would fit if the attendees' address books had been copied.","Correct. Sending unsolicited messages to discoverable Bluetooth devices is bluejacking. It is a nuisance rather than a data theft attack.","Incorrect. War driving surveys wireless networks while moving. It would fit if someone were logging SSIDs from a car outside."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"A visitor's phone connects to a discoverable Bluetooth device without any pairing prompt, and the owner later discovers her contacts and calendar were copied. What attack is this?",c:["Bluesnarfing of stored data","NFC skimming at close range","Radio jamming of the Bluetooth spectrum","Bluejacking of the visitor's phone"],a:0,e:["Correct. Unauthorized retrieval of contacts and calendar data over Bluetooth is bluesnarfing.","Incorrect. NFC works at a few centimeters and uses a different technology. It would fit if a payment card were read by a nearby reader.","Incorrect. Jamming disrupts communications rather than copying data. It would fit if the Bluetooth link had simply stopped working.","Incorrect. Bluejacking only sends messages and does not extract data. It would fit if the phone had merely received an unwanted pop-up."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"A new router ships with a vendor login that is printed in the public manual, and the administrator never changes it. An attacker reaches the management page and logs in on the first attempt. Which weakness MOST directly enabled the compromise?",c:["Vendor backdoor inserted in the supply chain","Default credentials left on the router","Management port exposed to the internet","Zero-day flaw in the router firmware"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A supply chain backdoor is hidden malicious code from the vendor. It would fit if the login worked only because the vendor hid an undocumented account.","Correct. Published factory credentials that were never changed gave access in one attempt. Changing them at deployment removes the weakness.","Incorrect. Exposure only gave the attacker a place to try, but the login succeeded because of the known password. It would be the answer if the page were reachable and no credential was needed.","Incorrect. A zero-day is an unknown, unpatched flaw. It would fit if the attacker bypassed authentication through a code bug."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"Attackers compromise a managed service provider's remote management tool and push ransomware to every client the MSP supports. Which vector does this represent?",c:["Watering hole infection via the MSP's public portal","Removable media plugged in at the MSP office","Typosquatted look-alike of the MSP's domain","Supply chain compromise via the MSP's tooling"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A watering hole infects visitors of a compromised site. It would fit if clients were infected only by browsing the MSP's public website.","Incorrect. No physical media is involved. It would fit if the MSP's technicians had plugged in infected drives.","Incorrect. Typosquatting depends on mistyped domains. It would fit if clients had been tricked into visiting a look-alike MSP address.","Correct. Compromising a trusted provider to reach its customers is a supply chain attack, and MSPs are valuable targets because they hold access to many clients."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"A user receives a collaboration chat from an external tenant claiming to be IT support, asking them to install a 'fix' from a link. Which threat vector is used?",c:["Voice-based vector via phone call","Removable-media vector via USB drive","Message-based vector via team chat","Image-based vector via embedded graphics"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Voice vectors involve calls or voicemail. It would fit if the same pretext were delivered by phone.","Incorrect. Removable media requires a physical device. It would fit if the installer had arrived on a dropped USB drive.","Correct. Instant messaging and collaboration platforms are message-based vectors, and attackers increasingly use them because users trust internal-looking chats.","Incorrect. Image-based vectors hide exploits in pictures. It would fit if the compromise came from a malicious image file."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"An executive wants email bodies encrypted end to end so mail administrators cannot read them, and signed so senders cannot deny authorship. Which technology satisfies this?",c:["S/MIME with X.509 certificates","STARTTLS between mail servers","DMARC policy with reporting","DKIM signing of outbound messages"],a:0,e:["Correct. S/MIME uses certificates to encrypt message content for the recipient and digitally sign it, giving confidentiality, integrity, and non-repudiation.","Incorrect. STARTTLS encrypts server-to-server hops only, so administrators can still read messages stored on servers. It would fit if the goal were protecting mail in transit between relays.","Incorrect. DMARC sets a policy and reports on SPF and DKIM results. It would fit if the goal were blocking domain spoofing.","Incorrect. DKIM authenticates the sending domain but does not encrypt the body or give per-user non-repudiation. It would fit if the goal were proving a message left the domain unmodified."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"A coffee shop's customers see two networks with the same SSID, one with a much stronger signal. Users who join it have their traffic captured, and the strong network is not managed by the shop. What is this?",c:["Rogue access point on the wired LAN","Evil twin imitation of a legitimate AP","Wireless jamming of the legitimate AP","Deauthentication-only denial of service"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A rogue AP is an unauthorized device plugged into the organization's network. It would fit if someone had connected an unapproved AP to the shop's switch.","Correct. An access point that copies a legitimate SSID to lure clients and capture traffic is an evil twin.","Incorrect. Jamming blocks the legitimate signal rather than lure users onto another network. It would fit if clients simply lost connectivity.","Incorrect. Deauthentication frames disconnect clients but do not capture their traffic. They are often used to push users toward an evil twin, but they would fit alone only if the goal were to knock users offline."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"Which description BEST matches pretexting?",c:["Registering look-alike domains to catch mistyped addresses","Redirecting DNS responses to fraudulent servers","Compromising sites that a target group habitually visits","Inventing a believable story to justify a request"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. That describes typosquatting. It would fit if the scenario involved a misspelled domain.","Incorrect. That describes pharming. It would fit if users typing correct URLs were sent to a fake server.","Incorrect. That describes a watering hole attack. It would fit if a trade website popular with the targets were infected.","Correct. Pretexting is the fabricated story, such as claiming to be an auditor who needs a vendor list by noon, used to make the target comply."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"A malicious PDF hosted on a public file-sharing site exploits a flaw in the reader application when a user opens it. Which threat vector does this represent?",c:["File-based vector via a document exploit","Image-based vector through embedded pictures","Voice-based vector through a phone call","Unsecure-network vector through open Wi-Fi"],a:0,e:["Correct. A booby-trapped document such as a PDF or Office file that exploits the application is a file-based vector.","Incorrect. Image-based vectors carry exploits in picture files such as SVG or PNG. It would fit if the payload were hidden in an image the user viewed.","Incorrect. No call is involved. It would fit if a caller had talked the user into installing software.","Incorrect. The network is not the weakness here. It would fit if an attacker were capturing traffic on an open hotspot."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"An attacker uploads a profile picture in SVG format to a forum. Script embedded in the image runs in the browser of every member who views the profile. Which vector is MOST specific?",c:["File-based vector through an Office macro","Message-based vector through direct chat","Image-based vector via an embedded script","Removable-device vector through a USB drive"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Office macros are document-based file vectors. It would fit if the payload were a macro inside a spreadsheet.","Incorrect. Message-based vectors use email, SMS, or chat. It would fit if the script were sent as a chat message.","Correct. SVG is an XML image format that can contain script, so the malicious content rides inside a picture. That makes it an image-based vector.","Incorrect. No physical media is involved. It would fit if the payload were delivered on a dropped USB stick."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"A vulnerability scan shows a public-facing server accepting connections on TCP 23 and TCP 21 although neither service is needed. Which attack surface weakness is this?",c:["Default credentials left on the admin console","Cleartext protocols on open service ports","Excessive permissions on service accounts","Unpatched firmware in a vendor appliance"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Default credentials are unchanged logins, and the scan reports nothing about passwords. It would fit if the scan showed admin/admin working.","Correct. Telnet and FTP are cleartext protocols, and leaving unneeded services open enlarges the attack surface. Disable them or replace them with SSH and SFTP.","Incorrect. Over-privileged service accounts relate to authorization. It would fit if a service ran as root with unnecessary rights.","Incorrect. Firmware patching concerns a version the vendor has fixed. It would fit if the scan identified an outdated build with known CVEs."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"The security team wants outside mail servers to reject messages that spoof the company's domain. Which configuration BEST accomplishes this?",c:["Publishing DKIM records with a DMARC policy of none","Enabling STARTTLS on the outbound mail relay","Scanning inbound attachments with a mail gateway sandbox","Publishing SPF and DKIM with a DMARC reject policy"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A DMARC policy of none only monitors and asks receivers to deliver spoofed mail anyway. It would fit an early phase of rollout when collecting reports.","Incorrect. STARTTLS encrypts the hop between servers but does nothing to prove the sender's domain. It would fit if the goal were protecting mail in transit.","Incorrect. Sandboxing scans mail coming into your organization, not what others receive from a spoofed domain. It would fit if the goal were stopping malware in inbound attachments.","Correct. SPF and DKIM let receivers verify the sender, and a DMARC reject policy tells them to discard failures, which blocks spoofing of your domain."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"A flyer in the break room advertises an 'IT Help Desk' hotline. Employees who call are asked to verify their username and password. The flyer offers nothing free and contains no link. Which technique is MOST clearly used?",c:["Impersonating IT support to build a pretext","Baiting with something tempting left behind for victims","Tailgating to enter the facility","Dumpster diving for discarded documents"],a:0,e:["Correct. Posing as the help desk to make victims hand over credentials is impersonation supported by a pretext.","Incorrect. Baiting uses a tempting item, such as a free USB drive, to lure victims. It would fit if the flyer had offered a free gift card for calling.","Incorrect. Tailgating is following an authorized person through a door. It would fit if the attacker were physically entering the building behind an employee.","Incorrect. Dumpster diving recovers information from trash. It would fit if the attacker were sifting through discarded printouts for passwords."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"A company's 'Our Team' page lists each employee's name, title, and direct email address. An attacker uses it to craft convincing messages to the finance department. Which risk does the page MOST directly increase?",c:["SQL injection through form inputs","On-path interception of the form submissions in transit","Spear phishing using public staff details","Buffer overflow in the web server"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. SQL injection exploits unsanitized database input. It would fit if the page contained a search box passing input to a database query.","Incorrect. On-path interception depends on network position, not on public staff data. It would fit if the page transmitted form data without TLS.","Correct. Names, titles, and emails make reconnaissance easy, allowing believable targeted spear phishing aimed at the right roles.","Incorrect. A buffer overflow is a memory flaw in software. It would fit if the page revealed an outdated web server build."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"Which set of controls BEST reduces the risk of a watering hole attack?",c:["Changing default router credentials and disabling unused admin services","Publishing SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records with a reject policy","Disabling Bluetooth discoverability and requiring pairing approval","Patching browsers and plugins, web filtering, and browser isolation"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Hardening router defaults addresses default credential attacks. It would fit if the threat were attackers logging into network gear.","Incorrect. Email authentication helps against domain spoofing. It would fit if the threat were forged mail from your domain.","Incorrect. Bluetooth controls address bluejacking and bluesnarfing. They would fit if the threat were proximity attacks on phones.","Correct. Watering holes typically exploit browser or plugin flaws on trusted sites, so patching, reputation filtering, and isolation reduce the impact."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"An attacker registers 'micros0ft-support.com' using a zero in place of the letter O and copies Microsoft's logos onto the page. Which TWO tactics are present?",c:["Pharming and voice-call vishing attacks","Typosquatting and brand impersonation","Watering hole and smishing text lures","Domain hijacking and pretext phone calls"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Pharming corrupts DNS and vishing uses voice calls, and neither appears here. They would fit if users typing the real address were redirected and a phone call followed.","Correct. A look-alike domain with a character swap is typosquatting, and reusing Microsoft's logos to look official is brand impersonation.","Incorrect. A watering hole compromises a genuine site, and smishing uses SMS. They would fit if a trusted site had been infected and a text lure sent.","Incorrect. Domain hijacking takes over an existing domain, whereas this one was newly registered. It would fit if Microsoft's real registration had been seized."]},
{o:"2.2",q:"Employees working from a hotel connect their laptops to the hotel's open Wi-Fi without using a VPN. Which attack surface does this create?",c:["Unsecure wireless network open to eavesdropping","Default credentials on the hotel's access point","Removable media inserted at the business center","Supply chain exposure through the hotel's IT vendor"],a:0,e:["Correct. An open network lacks encryption and authentication, so traffic can be eavesdropped or intercepted. A VPN protects the session.","Incorrect. Default credentials concern an unchanged login on the equipment. They would be the problem if an attacker accessed the AP's admin page.","Incorrect. Removable media requires a physical device. It would fit if the staff were using a USB drive from the business center printer.","Incorrect. Supply chain risk comes from vendor compromise. It would fit if malicious firmware had come with the hotel's wireless equipment."]},

// ---------- 2.3 Vulnerability types (28) ----------
{o:"2.3",q:"A site search page echoes the query back in the results header without encoding it. An attacker emails victims a link containing a script in the query string, and the script sends each victim's session cookie to the attacker. Nothing is saved on the server. What vulnerability is exploited?",c:["Reflected cross-site scripting (XSS)","Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)","Persistent cross-site scripting","Server-side request forgery (SSRF)"],a:0,e:["Correct. Unencoded input reflected into the page and delivered through a crafted link is reflected XSS, and stealing the session cookie is its classic outcome.","Incorrect. CSRF makes the victim's browser submit a state-changing request and does not run attacker script to steal cookies. It would fit if a hidden form changed the victim's email address.","Incorrect. Stored XSS saves the payload on the server and serves it to every visitor. It would fit if the script were posted to a comment field and ran for all viewers without clicking a link.","Incorrect. SSRF abuses the server into making requests on the attacker's behalf. It would fit if the query parameter made the server fetch internal URLs."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"An attacker posts a forum comment containing a script tag. The script is saved in the database and executes in the browser of every member who later opens the thread, with no link click required. What is this?",c:["Reflected cross-site scripting (XSS)","Session fixation on the forum login","Stored cross-site scripting (XSS)","Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Reflected XSS requires each victim to follow a crafted link, and nothing is stored. It would fit if the script ran only when a victim opened a malicious URL.","Incorrect. Session fixation forces a victim to use a session ID the attacker already knows. It would fit if the attacker set the session ID before the victim logged in.","Correct. A payload persisted on the server and served to all later visitors is stored (persistent) XSS.","Incorrect. CSRF forges a request from the victim's browser rather than running injected script. It would fit if a hidden form posted a transfer from another site."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A user who is logged in to an online bank opens a malicious page in another tab. The page silently submits a funds-transfer form to the bank, and the bank honors it because the browser attaches the session cookie. No script runs on the bank's site. What vulnerability is exploited?",c:["Stored cross-site scripting (XSS)","Session fixation before login","SQL injection in the transfer form","Cross-site request forgery attack"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Stored XSS runs attacker script within the vulnerable site. It would fit if the bank page itself executed a stored malicious script.","Incorrect. Fixation relies on the attacker pre-setting a session ID. It would fit if the victim had logged in using an ID supplied by the attacker.","Incorrect. SQL injection alters database queries through unsanitized input. It would fit if the transfer form's fields had changed the SQL statement.","Correct. The bank trusts any request carrying the user's session cookie, so a forged cross-site request succeeds. Anti-CSRF tokens and SameSite cookies are the defenses."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A login page builds its query as \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name='\" + input + \"'\", and an attacker authenticates by entering ' OR 1=1 --. Which fix addresses the ROOT cause?",c:["HTML output encoding for displayed values","Parameterized queries with bound input","Account lockout after repeated failed logins","Anti-CSRF tokens on every form"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Output encoding stops XSS in rendered pages. It would fit if the flaw were a script reflected into HTML rather than a modified query.","Correct. Bound variables keep input as data and never as SQL, eliminating this injection. Input validation adds depth.","Incorrect. Lockout slows password guessing, but this attacker logs in with one request. It would fit if the attack were brute-forcing credentials.","Incorrect. Anti-CSRF tokens stop forged cross-site requests. They would fit if the attack submitted unwanted actions from another site."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A C program copies user input into a fixed 64-byte array with no length check, and an attacker's longer input overwrites the saved return address to run injected code. What is this vulnerability?",c:["Heap memory leak from unreleased blocks","Integer overflow in a length counter","Stack buffer overflow in the copy routine","Race condition between two threads sharing data"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A memory leak is memory that is never released and leads to exhaustion. It would fit if the program slowly consumed RAM without any code execution.","Incorrect. Integer overflow occurs when arithmetic wraps past a numeric limit. It would fit if a length calculation wrapped around and led to a too-small allocation.","Correct. Writing past a fixed buffer and overwriting the return address is a stack buffer overflow, which can give code execution.","Incorrect. A race condition depends on timing between operations. It would fit if two threads corrupted shared data depending on execution order."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"An application verifies that a user may read a file, then opens it a moment later. In between, an attacker replaces the file with a symbolic link to a protected system file. What is this?",c:["Time-of-check to time-of-use race","Directory traversal using ../ sequences","Process memory injection into a service","Stack buffer overflow in the file parser"],a:0,e:["Correct. The resource changes between the permission check and its use, which is a TOCTOU race condition.","Incorrect. Traversal manipulates path strings to climb out of a directory. It would fit if the attacker passed ../../ in the filename.","Incorrect. Memory injection writes code into another process. It would fit if malware were loading itself into a running process.","Incorrect. A buffer overflow overwrites memory with oversized input. It would fit if the crash occurred after sending a long filename."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"An upload service scans a submitted file for malware, approves it, and moves it to a shared folder. An attacker swaps the file for a malicious one after the scan but before the move. Which term MOST specifically describes the flaw?",c:["Replay attack of captured uploads","TOCTOU race condition in the pipeline","Resource exhaustion of the upload server","Memory injection into the scanner"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Replay re-sends captured valid traffic. It would fit if the attacker re-submitted an earlier approved message.","Correct. The state changes in the window between the check (scan) and the use (move), which is the signature of a time-of-check to time-of-use race.","Incorrect. Exhaustion consumes CPU, memory, or disk to deny service. It would fit if thousands of uploads filled the storage.","Incorrect. Memory injection alters a running process's memory. It would fit if malicious code were written into the scanner process."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"Malware uses legitimate operating system APIs to write its code into the memory space of a trusted running process such as explorer.exe so it blends in. No crash or oversized input is involved. What is this?",c:["Stack buffer overflow in the process","TOCTOU race condition on a temp file","Firmware implant in the BIOS image","Process memory injection technique"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A buffer overflow requires oversized input corrupting memory. It would fit if the process crashed after receiving malformed data.","Incorrect. A race condition exploits timing between check and use. It would fit if a file were swapped between validation and open.","Incorrect. A firmware implant lives in device firmware and survives OS reinstalls. It would fit if the code persisted in a BIOS or router image.","Correct. Writing malicious code into another process's address space with normal APIs is memory (process) injection."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A vendor announced end of support for a model of network switch last year and publishes no more security fixes. New CVEs have since been disclosed for it. Which vulnerability category does this represent?",c:["Zero-day in the current firmware build","Misconfigured management plane settings","Unsupported end-of-life network hardware","Malicious update from the vendor's server"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A zero-day is a flaw unknown to the vendor with no patch yet. Here the flaws are publicly known and simply will never be fixed.","Incorrect. Misconfiguration is a setting error that an administrator can correct. It would fit if the switch's management interface were exposed by mistake.","Correct. Once a vendor stops patching, each new flaw stays open permanently, so end-of-life and legacy hardware must be replaced or isolated.","Incorrect. A malicious update is tampered vendor code. It would fit if the vendor's update server had been compromised."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A user downloads an APK from a website and installs it on a managed Android phone, bypassing the official app store. The OS itself has not been modified. What is this called?",c:["Side loading from an outside website","Jailbreaking the device's operating system","Rooting the device's operating system","Sandbox escape from a mobile app"],a:0,e:["Correct. Installing an app from outside the official store skips store vetting. That is side loading, and it does not require modifying the OS.","Incorrect. Jailbreaking removes iOS restrictions and applies to Apple devices. It would fit if an iPhone's security model had been bypassed.","Incorrect. Rooting grants administrative control over the Android OS. It would fit if the user had unlocked the bootloader to gain superuser access.","Incorrect. A sandbox escape is an app breaking out of its isolation. It would fit if the installed app exploited a flaw to access other apps' data."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"An iPhone owner exploits a flaw to remove Apple's software restrictions so unsigned apps and system modifications are possible. What is this called?",c:["Rooting the device to obtain administrator access","Jailbreaking the device's operating system","Side loading apps from a third-party website","Firmware downgrade to an older iOS release"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Rooting is the equivalent privilege gain on Android. It would fit if the device were an Android phone.","Correct. Removing iOS restrictions to gain elevated control is jailbreaking.","Incorrect. Side loading installs apps from outside the store without altering the OS. It would fit if the user installed a package but kept the OS intact.","Incorrect. A downgrade reinstalls an older firmware version. It would fit if the user reverted to a previous iOS release, which does not itself remove restrictions."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A tenant on a public cloud exploits a hypervisor bug to break out of its virtual machine and read the memory of another customer's VM on the same host. What is this?",c:["Resource reuse of unscrubbed storage","Insecure API with no rate limits","Misconfigured storage bucket policy","VM escape from a guest to the host"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Resource reuse involves leftover data in reallocated resources, with no isolation break. It would fit if a new VM received disk blocks holding an earlier tenant's data.","Incorrect. An insecure API lacks authentication or limits on an endpoint. It would fit if the attacker called a management function with no credentials.","Incorrect. A bucket misconfiguration exposes stored objects through permissions. It would fit if the data were readable on a public storage URL.","Correct. Breaking out of a guest to reach the hypervisor or neighboring guests is a VM escape, and the key mitigation is patching the hypervisor."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A newly provisioned cloud VM is given storage blocks that still contain a previous tenant's files, although isolation between tenants was never broken. Which vulnerability is this?",c:["Resource reuse after reallocation","VM escape from the hypervisor host","Race condition on shared storage","Insecure API on the storage service"],a:0,e:["Correct. Memory or disk that is not scrubbed before being reallocated exposes earlier data, which is resource reuse.","Incorrect. A VM escape breaks out of a running guest into the host. It would fit if the tenant had exploited the hypervisor to read another live VM.","Incorrect. A race condition depends on timing between operations. It would fit if two processes altered a resource in the gap between a check and its use.","Incorrect. An insecure API exposes an endpoint without proper authentication. It would fit if a management call returned data without credentials."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"An administrator accidentally sets an object storage bucket's access control to public read, exposing customer invoices. No software defect is involved. Which vulnerability type is this?",c:["Zero-day flaw in the storage service","Software supply chain compromise","Misconfiguration of access controls","Resource reuse by another tenant"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A zero-day is an unknown code flaw. It would fit if invoices were exposed because of an unpatched bug in the storage service.","Incorrect. A supply chain compromise poisons trusted software or a vendor. It would fit if a dependency had shipped with a backdoor.","Correct. A permissions setting error is a misconfiguration, which is one of the most common cloud weaknesses.","Incorrect. Resource reuse leaks leftover data between tenants. It would fit if the invoices were recovered from a reallocated disk."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"Attackers compromise the build pipeline of a popular open-source library so that a backdoor ships inside its signed releases, and thousands of downstream applications pick it up. What is this?",c:["Zero-day flaw in the operating system","Software supply chain compromise","Side loading of an unvetted app","Cloud permissions misconfiguration"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. An OS zero-day is an unknown flaw in the operating system. It would fit if the backdoor were an exploitable bug in the OS kernel.","Correct. Poisoning a trusted upstream dependency to reach every downstream user is a software supply chain vulnerability. SBOMs and verified builds help.","Incorrect. Side loading installs apps from outside an official store. It would fit if users installed a package from an untrusted website.","Incorrect. Cloud misconfiguration is a setting error. It would fit if a bucket or role were left overly permissive."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A router's firmware contains a hard-coded maintenance password that administrators cannot change through any setting. What is the BEST classification?",c:["Default credentials fixable by an administrator","Misconfigured ACL on the management interface","Weak password policy in the directory service","Firmware flaw that needs a vendor update"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Default credentials can be changed by the owner. It would fit if the login were a factory password that settings allow you to replace.","Incorrect. An ACL error can be fixed by editing the rule. It would fit if the management interface were open to the whole internet by mistake.","Incorrect. Password policy applies to accounts in the directory. It would fit if users were allowed short, reusable passwords.","Correct. The weakness is built into the code, so only a vendor firmware update or replacement removes it."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A researcher finds a web server flaw that the vendor does not yet know about, and attackers are already exploiting it. No patch exists. What is it called?",c:["Zero-day vulnerability in the server","Known CVE with an available patch","End-of-life vulnerability in the product","Misconfiguration of the web server"],a:0,e:["Correct. A flaw unknown to the vendor, with no patch available and active exploitation, is a zero-day.","Incorrect. A known CVE with a patch can be remediated by updating. It would fit if the vendor had published a fix that was not yet applied.","Incorrect. End-of-life means support has ended, so fixes will not come. It would fit if the vendor knew about the flaw but no longer patched the product.","Incorrect. Misconfiguration is a setting mistake rather than a code flaw. It would fit if the server were exposed because of a wrong permission."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"After a successful cross-site scripting attack, which outcome is MOST likely?",c:["Deletion of database tables on the server","Memory exhaustion that crashes the server","Theft of the victim's session cookie data","Modification of the server's DNS records"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Deleting tables is typical of SQL injection against the database. It would fit if unsanitized input had altered a SQL statement.","Incorrect. Exhausting memory is a denial-of-service or overflow issue. It would fit if the server received malformed or excessive input.","Correct. XSS runs in the victim's browser, so common results are stealing session data, redirecting the user, or defacing the page.","Incorrect. DNS changes require control of a DNS server or registrar. They would be the result of an attack on name resolution, not on web input handling."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"An attacker changes a URL from /download?file=report.pdf to /download?file=../../etc/passwd and receives the system's password file. What vulnerability is being exploited?",c:["Command injection in the shell wrapper","Forced browsing of unlinked admin pages","Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)","Directory traversal in the path handling"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Command injection runs operating system commands through input. It would fit if the parameter were appended to a shell command and executed.","Incorrect. Forced browsing guesses unlinked pages such as /admin. It would fit if the attacker were enumerating hidden paths rather than climbing directories.","Incorrect. CSRF forges requests from a victim's browser. It would fit if the victim were tricked into triggering the download.","Correct. The ../ sequences climb out of the intended directory, and input validation plus restrictive file permissions prevent it."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"Which pair of controls BEST makes a buffer overflow harder to turn into code execution?",c:["Output encoding and a content security policy","ASLR and non-executable memory (DEP/NX)","Anti-CSRF tokens and SameSite cookies","Full-disk encryption with a TPM"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Output encoding and CSP defend against XSS. They would fit if the flaw were unsanitized output in a web page.","Correct. ASLR randomizes memory layout and DEP/NX blocks execution from data pages, so injected code is hard to locate and run. Bounds checking is the real fix.","Incorrect. Anti-CSRF tokens and SameSite cookies defend against forged requests. They would fit if the attack abused a logged-in session.","Incorrect. Disk encryption protects data at rest if a device is stolen. It would fit if the threat were physical theft of a laptop."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"An MDM administrator wants to address weaknesses that are specific to mobile operating systems. Which pair should be covered?",c:["Side loading and jailbreaking","SQL injection and cross-site scripting","Firmware backdoors and hard-coded credentials","VM escape and resource reuse"],a:0,e:["Correct. Installing apps outside the store and removing OS restrictions (jailbreaking or rooting) are mobile-specific, and MDM policy can detect and block them.","Incorrect. These are web application flaws that affect any client. They would fit if the review covered a customer-facing portal.","Incorrect. These are device firmware problems typical of routers and IoT. They would fit if the assessment covered embedded network gear.","Incorrect. These are virtualization and cloud weaknesses. They would fit if the administrator were assessing a hypervisor environment."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A cloud management endpoint accepts requests from anyone and returns customer records because it never checks for an authentication token. Which vulnerability does this describe?",c:["Overly permissive IAM role assignment","Resource reuse of unscrubbed disks","Insecure API endpoint exposure","VM escape from a tenant guest"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An IAM role problem is a case of authenticated identities holding too many rights. It would fit if a valid service account could read data beyond its job.","Incorrect. Resource reuse leaks leftover data in reallocated resources. It would fit if a new VM received another tenant's old disk blocks.","Correct. An endpoint that skips authentication, and may also lack rate limits, is an insecure API, a leading cloud risk.","Incorrect. VM escape breaks out of a guest to the hypervisor. It would fit if a tenant had exploited a hypervisor flaw."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"Which practice represents a cryptographic vulnerability?",c:["Encrypting stored data with AES-256 and rotating keys","Hashing passwords with a salted adaptive function","Protecting data with deprecated DES and SHA-1","Negotiating TLS 1.3 with ephemeral key exchange"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. AES-256 with key rotation is current best practice. It would be a weakness only if the keys were stored beside the data.","Incorrect. Salted adaptive hashing such as bcrypt or Argon2 is the recommended way to store passwords. It would be a weakness if the salt were omitted.","Correct. Deprecated algorithms and short keys are cryptographic vulnerabilities because they are practical to break or collide.","Incorrect. TLS 1.3 with forward secrecy is a modern secure configuration. It would be a weakness only if older, broken ciphers were allowed."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A standard user can gain SYSTEM privileges through a flaw in a kernel driver. Which action is MOST effective at removing the vulnerability itself?",c:["Apply the vendor's security update for the driver","Remove local administrator rights from standard users","Deploy an EDR agent to alert on privilege escalation","Move the workstation to a separate VLAN"],a:0,e:["Correct. The flaw is in the driver code, so the vendor's update removes it. Compensating controls only limit exposure until it is installed.","Incorrect. The users are already standard users and the flaw lets them escalate anyway. Least privilege reduces impact but does not fix this driver defect.","Incorrect. EDR detects exploitation but leaves the flaw in place. It would be the better choice if no patch were available yet and detection were needed.","Incorrect. Segmentation limits lateral movement but the local escalation still works on the machine. It would be useful as a stopgap for unpatchable systems."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"Which scenario BEST illustrates a malicious update vulnerability?",c:["An administrator defers a security patch for several months after release","A patch fails to install on several servers because the disk is full","A firewall rule allows unneeded inbound traffic to a production server","A compromised vendor update server delivers malware as a trusted update"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Deferring patches leaves known flaws open, which is poor patch management. It would be the answer if the question were about unpatched software.","Incorrect. A failed install is an operational problem that leaves the system unpatched. It would fit a question on patch deployment failures.","Incorrect. An overly permissive rule is a misconfiguration. It would fit a question on excess network exposure.","Correct. The update channel itself delivers the malware, so customers trust and install it. Code signing and update integrity checks mitigate it."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"Which vulnerability is BEST mitigated by server-side input validation?",c:["VM escape from a hosted guest machine","Command injection through a form field","Hard-coded credentials in device firmware","Side loading of an unvetted mobile app"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. VM escape is a hypervisor flaw fixed by patching. Input validation on the application would not affect it.","Correct. Validating and sanitizing input on the server, together with parameterization, blocks attacker data from being interpreted as commands or queries.","Incorrect. Hard-coded credentials are removed by a vendor firmware update. They would be mitigated by validation only if they were typed in as input.","Incorrect. Side loading is controlled through MDM policy and device settings. It would not be affected by server-side validation."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"Two threads read the same account balance at the same time, each add a deposit, and write back, so one deposit is lost. What BEST describes this flaw?",c:["Stack buffer overflow in the code","Memory leak in the application","Integer overflow of the balance","Race condition between two threads"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A buffer overflow writes beyond allocated memory. It would fit if an oversized input corrupted adjacent data.","Incorrect. A memory leak is allocated memory never released. It would fit if the program grew until it ran out of RAM.","Incorrect. Integer overflow occurs when a value exceeds its numeric range. It would fit if the balance wrapped around to a negative number.","Correct. The outcome depends on the timing of concurrent operations on shared data, which is a race condition."]},
{o:"2.3",q:"A company runs IP cameras whose vendor has ended support and cannot be replaced this quarter. Which control BEST reduces the risk from their permanently unpatched firmware?",c:["Enable automatic firmware updates from the camera vendor","Isolate them on a VLAN with restricted outbound access","Install host-based antivirus and EDR on each camera","Increase the logging level and retention on each camera"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The vendor no longer releases updates, so there is nothing to download automatically. It would be the answer for supported devices.","Correct. Segmentation with tight egress filtering is a compensating control that limits what a compromised, unpatchable device can reach until replacement.","Incorrect. Embedded camera firmware generally cannot run endpoint agents. It would fit for general-purpose computers.","Incorrect. More logging detects problems but does not prevent exploitation or contain it. It would help as an added detective control."]}
]);
